Scalar function: returns a single value (e.g., calculate age). Table-valued function: returns a table (inline or multi-statement). Examples:
```sql
-- Scalar function
CREATE FUNCTION dbo.GetStudentAge(@StudentID INT)
RETURNS INT
AS
BEGIN
    DECLARE @Age INT;
    SELECT @Age = DATEDIFF(YEAR, DateOfBirth, GETDATE()) FROM Student WHERE StudentID = @StudentID;
    RETURN @Age;
END;
-- Inline table-valued function
CREATE FUNCTION dbo.GetCoursesByStudent(@StudentID INT)
RETURNS TABLE
AS
RETURN (
    SELECT c.CourseID, c.Title, e.Grade
    FROM Enrollment e
    JOIN Course c ON e.CourseID = c.CourseID
    WHERE e.StudentID = @StudentID
);
-- Usage: SELECT * FROM dbo.GetCoursesByStudent(101);
```

T-SQL statements for user creation, role assignment, permission granting in SQL Server:
```sql
-- Create server login (authentication)
CREATE LOGIN JohnDoe WITH PASSWORD = 'Jd@2024!', DEFAULT_DATABASE = UniversityDB;
-- Create database user
USE UniversityDB;
CREATE USER JohnDoe FOR LOGIN JohnDoe;
-- Create custom role
CREATE ROLE DataEntryRole;
-- Grant permissions to role
GRANT INSERT, UPDATE ON Student TO DataEntryRole;
GRANT SELECT ON Enrollment TO DataEntryRole;
-- Assign user to role
EXEC sp_addrolemember 'DataEntryRole', 'JohnDoe';
-- Additional: Grant schema-level permission
GRANT EXECUTE ON SCHEMA::dbo TO DataEntryRole;
-- Deny delete
DENY DELETE ON Student TO DataEntryRole;
```
Use fixed roles: `EXEC sp_addrolemember 'db_datareader', 'JohnDoe';` To revoke: `REVOKE SELECT ON Student FROM DataEntryRole;`

